4. Results
We have tested our method on real human hand motion sequences. The polygonal skin of the 3D hand model totally has 1114 vertices and 2224 triangular faces. The depth maps are acquired at 16 frames per second with the image resolution of 320×240 pixels. The following experiments were executed on a Pentium 3 1.6GHz PC. In our experiments, a fully expanded hand region in the depth map contains roughly 20,000 points. The hand region is divided into small grids each containing 30 to 50 measurements. The data are sub-sampled at 500 stochastically determined points. The inner-loop in Figure 6 takes at most 30 iterations. The fitting speed is about 0.6 second per frame with 300 iterations in total for our method. The optimization algorithm can be made multi-threaded by dividing the x-y plane into unoverlapped blocks based on the computing resource. The performance enhancement of a multi-threaded version depends on the number of CPUs that, in turn, determine how many blocks can be processed in parallel. We also tested a fully paralleled version running on a dual Xeon 3.6GHz multi-core system, and achieved the speed of 0.1 second per frame.

