5. Conclusion
Recovering finger positions in a dynamic 3D virtual keyboard is a difficult problem due to the large number of degrees of freedom involved. We have described a fast 3D hand model fitting method that can perform the task in less than 100ms per pose starting from a 3D image sequence. To speed up the fitting process, a simplified physical based model is employed. Our experiments show that this fitting method performs robustly and efficiently even for some complex selfocclusion cases.
Further research will be carried out in several directions: a pre-calibration process to adjust the 3D hand model to any user with different hand shape / size is a must for a practical virtual keyboard system. The hand motion tracking with the training and analysis of normal human typing movement [12] is needed to estimate the model deformation parameters and will ultimately enhance the optimization performance. Using of an implicit expression [13] for the skin surface is also being studied. This method will simplify this non-rigid discontinuous optimization problem to a well-functioned, differentiable solution.

